<p>NASA has ruled out a March 2026 launch for Artemis II, the first crewed mission of its Artemis lunar program, after workers in Florida spotted an interruption in helium flow to the Space Launch System rocket’s upper stage. NASA now expects the launch to slip beyond March. Officials have discussed April as the next possible opportunity, while teams troubleshoot and decide whether they need to roll the fully stacked rocket and Orion spacecraft back into the Vehicle Assembly Building for deeper access and repairs. Artemis II will send astronauts around the Moon and back, a key rehearsal for a later landing attempt. Several outlets note Artemis III is currently planned for 2028.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/SrAXBK0Lji9i0dNnrJwSDlI0ZwWqVPfJpkipLQIaV-XZIaRIcjbYuMztGVIy-ct3hWTw1u3mM8kdLTGeeMOtj_jEuFKQr4I-s0rs68gOVszf7R4rz6r0a4oH5f8yCCq6l_8hFd5nPlGNkPXzf0C1MMhqjZBYs_1hFzLh7yor3dDGee6LnZHGxufF2U9ga7bXhvKC4gIl-JxJbWHHwb9I3F4PD1HjL6n54Z6YIWc7a9_hwrht5-4ZaF9IY-VMVERXwnM_mvWjcHTxGxgEmg' alt='File photo: The Space Launch System (SLS) with the Orion crew capsule at launch complex 39B during a wet dress rehearsal for Artemis II at Kennedy Space Center, Florida, Feb. 2, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>When NASA shared the news: The agency disclosed the helium-flow interruption on Saturday, a day after it publicly pointed to March 6 as a target date.</li><li>Next steps: Teams are weighing whether they can fix the issue at the pad or whether they need to move the integrated rocket and Orion back to the Vehicle Assembly Building for better access.</li><li>How NASA is describing the schedule now: Officials have moved from a specific March target to saying April is simply the next possible opportunity, depending on what troubleshooting turns up.</li><li>Where troubleshooting stands: Engineers still have not identified the source of the helium-flow interruption.</li><li>Why it matters: The slip affects what is expected to be the first crewed lunar-distance flight in decades, adding pressure to validate SLS and Orion performance ahead of later Artemis missions.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>NASA leadership: NASA’s chief said the agency is assessing the helium-flow problem, and that the next feasible launch opportunity would be no earlier than April. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/united-states/nasa-chief-rules-out-march-launch-of-moon-mission-over-technical-issue'>The Straits Times</a>)</li><li>NASA operations team: NASA said it is considering steps that could include rolling the rocket and Orion spacecraft back to the Vehicle Assembly Building at Kennedy Space Center to fix the issue. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/feb/21/nasa-artemis-ii-rocket-launch'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>BBC reporting: The BBC framed the delay as a safety-first move for the crew’s Moon flyby mission after engineers spotted a rocket issue. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c626v265zqlo'>BBC News</a>)</li><li>Deutsche Welle reporting: Deutsche Welle noted that NASA has not yet found the source of the problem, highlighting the uncertainty behind the revised schedule. (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/us-technical-issues-force-nasa-to-postpone-moon-mission/a-76075479'>Deutsche Welle</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-artemis-ii-launch-updates-nasa/'>NASA’s Artemis II launch hits new problem, putting March date in jeopardy</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li><li><a href='https://www.firstpost.com/world/nasa-delays-artemis-ii-moon-mission-scraps-march-launch-window-over-technical-issue-13982380.html'>Nasa delays Artemis II Moon mission, scraps March launch window over technical issue</a> - firstpost.com</li><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/2/21/nasa-rules-out-march-launch-for-manned-moon-mission-over-technical-issues'>NASA rules out March launch for manned moon mission over technical issues</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://www.b